[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So is this going to be the procedure that finally&nbsp; replaces LASIC? This is called electromechanical&nbsp;&nbsp; reshaping and it has the potential to fix your&nbsp; aigmatism, nearsightedness or far-sightedness,&nbsp;&nbsp; all without using any lasers or vaporizing or&nbsp; cutting into the cornea. So to understand how this&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'works, first the cornea, the clear dome window&nbsp; on the surface of the eye, this is mostly made&nbsp;&nbsp; up of water and protein called collagen. Now the&nbsp; shape of that cornea is what focuses light. And&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'in fact a lot of people have coral aigmatism where&nbsp; that cornea might have a certain curve to it in a&nbsp;&nbsp; certain spot that causes a stigmatism blur. Now&nbsp; what LASIC does it uses a laser to vaporize the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'corneal tissue into a new shape allowing this new&nbsp; shape to correct your vision. Now, how EMR works,&nbsp;&nbsp; first step is they use a device that passes a very&nbsp; low-level, very safe electrical current through&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[00:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the corial tissue. Now, this isn\'t supposed to&nbsp; hurt, at least that\'s what they say, but it does&nbsp;&nbsp; temporarily change or loosen the collagen in the&nbsp; corial tissue by changing the water balance in the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'tissue. This makes the cornea softer, more&nbsp; pliable, more moldable. And then step two,&nbsp;&nbsp; they place a custom disc, kind of like a contact&nbsp; lens, over the surface of the eye that has the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'mold to the cornea that it needs. It pushes onto&nbsp; the cornea and the cornea molds to that disc,&nbsp;&nbsp; changing the shape of the cornea. Now, it sits&nbsp; there for a couple minutes and then when the&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'procedure is done, the corneal stiffens back to&nbsp; its original stiffened state and then it stays&nbsp;&nbsp; in that shape that it was molded to. Now, in a&nbsp; way, this is kind of already being done today&nbsp;&nbsp; through what\'s called ortho K contact lenses.&nbsp; These are hard, rigid gas permeable lenses that&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'people sleep in overnight. And that contact is&nbsp; made to reshape the cornea while they\'re asleep.&nbsp;&nbsp; And then when they wake up, they take the contact&nbsp; lens out. And because that cornea is reshaped,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[01:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'it corrects their vision and they walk around all&nbsp; day without wearing glasses or contact lenses. Now&nbsp;&nbsp; the downside to this is that cornea does slowly&nbsp; go back to its original shape and so they have&nbsp;&nbsp; to put the contact lenses in either every night&nbsp; or every other night depending on the person.&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:10] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'But in a way this is already being done today.&nbsp; This is just the next step where they can use an&nbsp;&nbsp; electrical current loosen the tissue to make the&nbsp; cornea more permanently molded like that. Now, I&nbsp;&nbsp; probably shouldn\'t use the word permanent because&nbsp; this is a procedure what they say can be redone,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'can be reversed. If down the road if the vision&nbsp; changes or the cornea does change shape, then they&nbsp;&nbsp; can go back, they can reshape it by doing the same&nbsp; procedure. It\'s supposed to be more affordable&nbsp;&nbsp; than LASIC and is an inoffice procedure. So, there&nbsp; are some very promising outcomes if this does&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'become a thing. Now, one possibly con is they are&nbsp; showing that it can possibly correct up to three&nbsp;&nbsp; diopters. So whether you\'re -3 + 3, which isn\'t a&nbsp; lot. Lasic can definitely do a lot more than that&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[02:57] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'depending on your corial thickness. And so there&nbsp; are limitations if that\'s the only amount that&nbsp;&nbsp; they\'ll be able to correct. I\'m kind of curious&nbsp; how much a stigmatism they\'d be able to correct&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:09] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'with that as well. So there might be a lot of&nbsp; people that might not be candidates for this, but&nbsp;&nbsp; we\'ll just have to see once this really becomes&nbsp; a procedure. But because this is non-invasive,&nbsp;&nbsp; meaning it\'s not like LASIC, where they use a&nbsp; laser to cut and vaporize the corial tissue,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'then we can potentially avoid a lot of the side&nbsp; effects that we get from LASIC. A big one being&nbsp;&nbsp; dry eye, permanent dry eye and nerve damage that&nbsp; some people suffer from and it can be very bad&nbsp;&nbsp; for them. And so this is why this is a good&nbsp; potential alternative to LASIK up and coming&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:41] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that a lot of people are excited about. Now,&nbsp; where are we in the process of this? They are&nbsp;&nbsp; able to show stability in lab settings on rabbits,&nbsp; rabbit eyes. They haven\'t done any human trials,&nbsp;&nbsp; but because it\'s looking promising, that\'s&nbsp; probably going to be the next step here soon. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;'

[03:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'I\'m crossing my fingers that the human trials&nbsp; go well. If you want to look into current&nbsp;&nbsp; alternatives to LASIC that you can do right&nbsp; now, check out this video and stay focused.'
